@@s001 From loop to recursion
@@s002 The same question, <b>factorial(5)</b>, with the two versions. Each rectangle is a call still open (a frame in the stack).
@@s003 On the left the call cannot close: it must wait for <code>factorial(4)</code> to do the multiplication. On the right <code>f</code> passes the work already done in the accumulator and <b>waits for nothing</b>: the new call takes the place of the old one.
@@s004 On the left the stack grows. On the right only one frame remains: <code>acc</code> is already worth 5 × 4 = 20.
@@s005 It continues the same.
@@s006 Fifth call on the left. On the right <code>acc</code> is worth 120: the result is already ready.
@@s007 Base case in both. On the right <b>I am done</b>: I return 120. On the left I only have the first number: now I must go back and do all the multiplications left pending.
@@s008 One call closes at a time: 2 × 1.
@@s009 3 × 2.
@@s010 4 × 6.
@@s011 5 × 24 = 120. Same result, but the normal version has kept open up to <b>n</b> calls: with n = 100 000 the stack runs out (<code>StackOverflowError</code>). The tail version always uses <b>one</b>.
@@s012 dato:state of the variables (while),ora:arguments of the call,ok:result
@@s013 due:multiplication pending,ok:value computed
@@s014 no:repeated calculation,dato:curr,ora:next,ok:result
@@s015 ora:pair (i, i+1) checked,ok:pair in order,no:pair out of order
@@s016 From loop to recursion
@@s017 Without <code>var</code> and without <code>for</code>, you repeat a calculation with a function that <b>calls itself</b> on a smaller problem. Here you turn a loop into recursion one step at a time, and see when the recursion is "tail".
@@s018 The recipe: from <code>while</code> to recursion
@@s019 It is the file <code>01-intro/Purification.scala</code>. You start from an imperative factorial and "purify" it. Follow the colours: each variable becomes a parameter.
@@s020 Purifying a loop
@@s021 dato:variables that change,ora:what I transform now,ok:pure version
@@s022 The starting point. Two variables change at each turn: <code>result</code> and <code>x</code>.
@@s023 <b>1.</b> Each variable that changes becomes a <b>parameter</b> of a working function. The return type stays <code>Int</code>.
@@s024 
@@s025 <b>2.</b> The condition of the <code>while</code> becomes an <code>if</code>: "must I do one more turn, or am I done?"
@@s026 <b>3.</b> The body of the loop computed the <b>new values</b> of the variables. Instead of assigning them, I pass them to the next call.
@@s027 <b>4.</b> After the loop there was <code>return result</code>: it becomes the <code>else</code> branch, without <code>return</code>.
@@s028 <b>5.</b> I hide <code>doWork</code> inside <code>factorial</code> and call it with the <b>initial values</b> of the variables: <code>result = 1</code>, <code>x = n</code>.
@@s029 Changing variables → arguments of the call
@@s030 The simplest version, and how it unrolls
@@s031 For the factorial there is also a direct recursion, copied from the mathematical definition <span class="m">n! = n · (n − 1)!</span>:
@@s032 factorial(4), substituting each call
@@s033 4 is not ≤ 1: <code>else</code> branch.
@@s034 The multiplication by 4 <b>remains pending</b>: first <code>factorial(3)</code> is needed.
@@s035 Another pending multiplication.
@@s036 And another.
@@s037 Base case: 1 ≤ 1, it is 1.
@@s038 Now the multiplications are closed, from inside outwards.
@@s039 The number of things pending grows with <code>n</code>: the stack uses space proportional to <code>n</code>.
@@s040 factorial(4): the stack grows, then closes
@@s041 Tail recursion
@@s042 Definition
@@s043 A call is <b>in tail</b> (in <i>tail position</i>) when the caller returns its result straight away, <b>without doing anything more on it</b>.
@@s044 Not in tail
@@s045 After the call there remains to multiply by <code>n</code>.
@@s046 In tail
@@s047 The result of the call <b>is</b> the result. The multiplication has already been done, in the argument <code>acc</code>.
@@s048 The trick to go from one to the other has a name: the <b>accumulator parameter</b>. Instead of combining the results while you go back, you combine them while you go forward and carry the partial result with you.
@@s049 The stack of calls: normal against tail
@@s050 The compiler turns a tail recursion into a loop, with constant space. With <code>@annotation.tailrec</code> you ask it to <b>check</b> that it really is tail: if it is not, it does not compile.
@@s051 In the course
@@s052 The exercise sheet says it clearly: do not insist on tail recursion if it is not required. The course prefers simplicity. It is needed when there is a real risk of running out of stack.
@@s053 Fibonacci: why <code>@tailrec</code> did not accept your version
@@s054 In the first version of exercise 3 there were <b>two</b> recursive calls added together. The compiler rejects it:
@@s055 
@@s056 The solution: to compute the next number you need <b>only the last two</b>. Carry them with you as parameters and go on.
@@s057 fib with a window of two values
@@s058 <code>curr</code> is the current Fibonacci number, <code>next</code> the one right after. They are the sliding window.
@@s059 One starts from F1 = 0 and F2 = 1, as the exercise text says.
@@s060 Each step moves the window by one: the new <code>curr</code> is the old <code>next</code>, the new <code>next</code> is the sum of the two. <code>n</code> counts how many moves are missing.
@@s061 When no moves are missing, <code>curr</code> is the answer. The call at the previous step is in tail: <code>@tailrec</code> is satisfied.
@@s062 fib(6): the sliding window
@@s063 Start: curr = 0, next = 1.
@@s064 curr takes 1 (the old next), next = 1 + 0.
@@s065 next = 1 + 1.
@@s066 next = 2 + 1.
@@s067 next = 3 + 2.
@@s068 n ≤ 1: I return curr. The sequence was 0, 1, 1, 2, 3, <b>5</b>: the sixth number.
@@s069 Two calls added together against a sliding window
@@s070 A loop over the indices: <code>isSorted</code>
@@s071 Exercise 4: do with recursion what you would do with a <code>for</code> over the indices. It is your solution.
@@s072 isSorted, exercise 4 of week 1
@@s073 The working function has only one parameter: the index <code>i</code>, which in a <code>for</code> would be the loop variable.
@@s074 If there is no pair <code>(i, i+1)</code> to check, the array is sorted. It also holds for empty arrays and for one-element arrays.
@@s075 I compare the element with the next one using the function received. If the pair does not respect the order, I answer <code>false</code> at once.
@@s076 Otherwise I go on to the next pair. The call is in tail.
@@s077 The loop starts from index 0.
@@s078 Frequent doubt
@@s079 <b>Why is it enough to compare the adjacent pairs?</b> If <code>as(0) ≤ as(1)</code> and <code>as(1) ≤ as(2)</code>, then also <code>as(0) ≤ as(2)</code>: the order propagates. Checking all the pairs would be useless.
@@s080 go(i): the for over the indices as recursion
